WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.270 --> 00:00:08.710
Roger command, request navigation...

00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:15.900
Imagine this: your aircraft has state of the
art avionics.

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:17.850
But your map is from the 15th century.

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:21.780
Command, this chart says we’re going to
go over the edge of the earth please advise,

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:22.780
please advise.

00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:25.750
Neuroscientists are stuck in this harrowing
predicament as they try to make their way

00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:27.190
around the human brain.

00:00:27.190 --> 00:00:31.750
The first classical map made about a century
ago is still widely used today.

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:37.710
Published in 1909, it defined regions on the
pinkish grey organ that control our actions

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:38.870
and functions.

00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:43.500
The fundamental unit of brain organization
for the cerebral cortex is what we call a

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:44.649
cortical area.

00:00:44.649 --> 00:00:48.650
So on the map you could say there’s a country
called ‘Speech’ and a country called ‘Short-term

00:00:48.650 --> 00:00:51.810
memory,’ another, ‘Hand movement.’

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:57.790
Identifying every one of these cortical areas
became a major objective of the Human Connectome

00:00:57.790 --> 00:01:02.280
Project, the HCP, needing the effort of a
master mapmaker.

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:04.960
I consider myself a cortical cartographer.

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:11.070
By July of last year, the HCP completed its
first phase: a 21st century world map of the

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:12.070
brain.

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:15.710
We reported the presence of 180 distinct cortical
areas.

00:01:15.710 --> 00:01:19.229
97 of them were new to brain science.

00:01:19.229 --> 00:01:24.259
The Connectome gives us this opportunity,
a really great tool to be able to navigate

00:01:24.259 --> 00:01:25.999
the human brain.

00:01:25.999 --> 00:01:29.579
Navigating the brain is more than just naming
cities and states.

00:01:29.579 --> 00:01:33.240
You need to know about the connections between
them.

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:39.450
The word ‘connectome’ implies that the
fundamentally important thing about brains

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:40.549
is connections.

00:01:40.549 --> 00:01:45.969
This evening, we’ll be talking and learning
about two different kinds of connections.

00:01:45.969 --> 00:01:49.819
Structural connections in the brain versus
functional connections in the brain.

00:01:49.819 --> 00:01:52.789
By structural connections, we’re really
talking about the physical connections.

00:01:52.789 --> 00:01:55.509
The wires that connect nerve cells.

00:01:55.509 --> 00:02:00.609
So, structural connectivity looks at the regions
and the pathways connecting them.

00:02:00.609 --> 00:02:05.469
Functional connectivity though is more about
how different parts of the brain work together

00:02:05.469 --> 00:02:07.239
on an ongoing basis.

00:02:07.239 --> 00:02:12.040
Brain function arises from conversations that
different brain regions are having with each

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:13.040
other.

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:19.220
A functional map must track living data, conversations
flowing through the pathways between brain

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:20.220
regions.

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:21.930
We collected data from 1200 individuals.

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:29.300
We had them do different tasks in the scanner,
things like memory tasks, emotions on faces.

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:32.590
Those same regions of the brain seem to be
forming networks.

00:02:32.590 --> 00:02:41.000
Brain structure is wired by it’s experience
but we have no idea in what form that wiring

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:42.870
diagram has that information.

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:45.550
And that’s the big puzzle.

00:02:45.550 --> 00:02:50.500
To see how that translates into individual
nerve connections, you’d need to look much

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:53.480
closer than David or Deanna’s technology
can go.

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:56.330
Down to the level of individual neurons.

00:02:56.330 --> 00:02:58.890
Two quadrillion times smaller.

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:02.850
What exactly is two quadrillion times smaller?

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:06.350
Well if the scale of David’s brain map is
the big picture.

00:03:06.350 --> 00:03:08.380
Equivalent to a map of the whole earth...

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:13.740
Then the area that Jeff’s covering would
be a city about the size of say, Sheboygan

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:14.740
Wisconsin.

00:03:14.740 --> 00:03:19.180
That little blur that little blob that there
might be Sheboygan, or we’re not sure.

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:22.730
That little blob is a one-millimeter cube
of brain tissue.

00:03:22.730 --> 00:03:25.740
And it contains a staggering number of neurons.

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:31.230
50 thousand nerve cells and about one billion
synapses.

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:38.380
So right now there’s no to model an entire
human brain by diagramming its micro-wiring.

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:42.870
Scientifically for now, we’re lost in the
foggy limits of brain science.

00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:52.801
You would need more digital information than
is the actual digital content of the world.

00:03:52.801 --> 00:03:59.280
We are going to have the most wonderful scientists
in the world talk to us about their experience

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:07.400
with mapping, with mapmaking, with map conceptualizing,
with brain understanding.

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:13.580
Our first guest is Professor of Neurobiology
at Washington University in St. Louis, Principal

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:17.329
investigator for the Human Connectome Project.

00:04:17.329 --> 00:04:21.189
A fellow of the American Association for the
Advancement of Science, he is internationally

00:04:21.189 --> 00:04:26.539
known for his research on the structure, functions,
connectivity, evolution, and development of

00:04:26.539 --> 00:04:38.659
the cerebral cortex in human and nonhuman
primates, please welcome David Van Essen.

00:04:38.659 --> 00:04:45.599
Our next participant is a Professor of Psychological
and Brain Sciences at Washington University

00:04:45.599 --> 00:04:49.110
St. Louis and an investigator with the Human
Connectome Project.

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:54.099
She’s on the scientific board of the Brain
and Behavior Research Foundation and the Stanley

00:04:54.099 --> 00:04:55.099
Foundation.

00:04:55.099 --> 00:04:59.710
A fellow of the Association for Psychological
Science and a member of the American College

00:04:59.710 --> 00:05:05.669
of Neuropsychopharmacology, please welcome
Deanna Barch.

00:05:05.669 --> 00:05:15.140
Joining us is a professor of molecular and
cellular biology and the Ramón y Cajal Professor

00:05:15.140 --> 00:05:18.020
of the Faculty of Arts and Sciences at Harvard
University.

00:05:18.020 --> 00:05:28.469
Developmental Neurobiologist, member of the
National Academy of Sciences, Jeff Lichtman.

00:05:28.469 --> 00:05:32.740
Our final guest is an associate professor
of psychology at Columbia University and director

00:05:32.740 --> 00:05:36.229
of the Developmental Affective Neuroscience
Laboratory.

00:05:36.229 --> 00:05:41.999
She’s a recipient of the Institution of
Mental Health Bio-Behavioral Research Award

00:05:41.999 --> 00:05:46.599
for Innovative Science, the American Psychological
Association’s distinguished Scientific award

00:05:46.599 --> 00:05:52.139
for early career contributions to psychology
and the Developmental Science Early Career

00:05:52.139 --> 00:05:56.969
Researcher Prize, Nim Tottenham.

00:05:56.969 --> 00:06:04.419
Jeff, what’s the Connectome?

00:06:04.419 --> 00:06:10.590
Well, on this stage you have four people who
would probably have a slightly different idea

00:06:10.590 --> 00:06:11.839
of what the Connectome is.

00:06:11.839 --> 00:06:12.889
That’s what we like.

00:06:12.889 --> 00:06:19.839
Yeah, so from my perspective, the Connectome
is a project to map the connections between

00:06:19.839 --> 00:06:25.270
the nerve cells and synapses, at the level
of synapses which is doable but the price

00:06:25.270 --> 00:06:31.669
we pay is this extraordinary amount of data
we need so that doing a cubic millimeter is

00:06:31.669 --> 00:06:35.969
for us a triumph, and we’re not done with
a cubic millimeter I should say.

00:06:35.969 --> 00:06:40.159
That’s two thousand terabytes of raw data.

00:06:40.159 --> 00:06:42.740
That’s not a zip drive, that’s--

00:06:42.740 --> 00:06:49.740
Yeah, but in terms of the other realms, and
maybe you guys can talk about this, a cubic

00:06:49.740 --> 00:06:51.889
millimeter is your voxel size.

00:06:51.889 --> 00:06:55.439
That’s the smallest thing you ever see.

00:06:55.439 --> 00:07:00.880
Nim, what do you-- how would you describe
the Connectome project to someone who asked

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:06.680
you, “so what do you do” at a party?

00:07:06.680 --> 00:07:11.669
I think, building on that answer, there are
so many levels to address when you’re thinking

00:07:11.669 --> 00:07:13.999
about connections in the brain.

00:07:13.999 --> 00:07:20.529
So there’s the very, very small levels that
have tremendous meaning and contain an incredible

00:07:20.529 --> 00:07:26.150
amount of information, but then you can look
at more macro levels that we can use with

00:07:26.150 --> 00:07:30.279
FMRI that tell us about a different level
of representation of information within the

00:07:30.279 --> 00:07:31.360
brain.

00:07:31.360 --> 00:07:37.119
So it’s going to take a lot of data to be
able to map out all of those various levels.

00:07:37.119 --> 00:07:39.879
And Deanna, how would you describe the Connectome?

00:07:39.879 --> 00:07:43.439
I think I would agree with Nim in the sense
that there’s really connectomes at many

00:07:43.439 --> 00:07:47.759
levels of analysis: at the level that Jeff
is working on, and then at the level that

00:07:47.759 --> 00:07:52.949
says ‘okay, all those different cubic millimeters
in the brain, how are they wired up and how

00:07:52.949 --> 00:07:58.700
do they work together, and how do all of those
conversations, to use a term Nim used, kind

00:07:58.700 --> 00:08:04.479
of support all the different behaviors, and
thinking and emotions that humans are able

00:08:04.479 --> 00:08:05.699
to do?

00:08:05.699 --> 00:08:09.749
So I think we really need all of those across
all of those scales eventually to really understand

00:08:09.749 --> 00:08:11.059
the full human connectome.

00:08:11.059 --> 00:08:16.699
And David, would it be fair to say, and I
deliberately left you last here, that you’re

00:08:16.699 --> 00:08:19.580
the most macro here?

00:08:19.580 --> 00:08:25.710
I think it’s fair to say that Deanna and
Nim all work at a very macroscopic level,

00:08:25.710 --> 00:08:33.070
the luxury of working on the entire brain
of living individuals but, and the other part

00:08:33.070 --> 00:08:38.950
of what drives us is the need for not only
as much information as we can but as rich

00:08:38.950 --> 00:08:44.530
across different types of imaging, magnetic
resonance imaging is the dominant one that

00:08:44.530 --> 00:08:48.200
we’ll talk about here but there are other,
what we call modalities, magnetoencephalography,

00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:56.000
electroencephalography, give a richer information
in the rapid time course of the signals they

00:08:56.000 --> 00:09:00.660
follow, but not as good in terms of the resolution
and space.

00:09:00.660 --> 00:09:06.350
So all of these types of information can work
hand in glove to give us deeper insights into

00:09:06.350 --> 00:09:10.150
an incredibly challenging and exciting set
of problems.

00:09:10.150 --> 00:09:11.150
Jeff.

00:09:11.150 --> 00:09:19.870
I mean, in many ways, you’re the astronaut
in the suit, sort of untethered from the mothership,

00:09:19.870 --> 00:09:24.530
way out there.

00:09:24.530 --> 00:09:27.760
What inspired you to do the work that you
do?

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:35.850
You know the history of neurobiology began
with this guy, Cajal who made this rather

00:09:35.850 --> 00:09:44.500
remarkable leap of faith from looking at structure
that has more or less stood the test of time,

00:09:44.500 --> 00:09:48.200
this idea of a directional network.

00:09:48.200 --> 00:09:56.500
And I think that just as going from Gregor
Mendel and his peas to the atomic structure

00:09:56.500 --> 00:10:03.060
of DNA to the human genome, going down and
then back up again, I think neurobiology has

00:10:03.060 --> 00:10:04.060
to do the same thing.

00:10:04.060 --> 00:10:09.630
You have these ideas that nerve cells are
connected in a wiring diagram, that’s what

00:10:09.630 --> 00:10:11.050
Cajal said.

00:10:11.050 --> 00:10:16.410
And now it’s up to our generation and the
generations following to get those wiring

00:10:16.410 --> 00:10:19.590
diagrams, to really know what they are.

00:10:19.590 --> 00:10:25.030
Many people would say the birth of neuroscience
came with the absolutely groundbreaking work

00:10:25.030 --> 00:10:34.150
of Santiago Ramón y Cajal, a Spanish physician,
pathologist, and ultimately neuroscientist,

00:10:34.150 --> 00:10:40.570
who used a technique called the Golgi stain,
that allowed Cajal to stain brains in ways

00:10:40.570 --> 00:10:46.330
that very small numbers of nerve cells would
be rendered dark and all the other cells would

00:10:46.330 --> 00:10:47.760
be clear.

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:51.800
Cajal was a gifted visual genius.

00:10:51.800 --> 00:10:55.530
He had ink, a paintbrush.

00:10:55.530 --> 00:10:56.770
That’s what he did.

00:10:56.770 --> 00:11:00.430
He just drew pictures on paper.

00:11:00.430 --> 00:11:04.210
They are absolutely beautiful.

00:11:04.210 --> 00:11:10.870
And not only beautiful, but he inferred this
very dynamic view of the way information flows.

00:11:10.870 --> 00:11:16.210
There’s a network of cells and the cells
are of various types and they talk to each

00:11:16.210 --> 00:11:17.210
other.

00:11:17.210 --> 00:11:20.420
And he was right almost always.

00:11:20.420 --> 00:11:25.770
And it was such a radical idea that Golgi,
who invented the stain, absolutely did not

00:11:25.770 --> 00:11:31.170
believe that that was true.

00:11:31.170 --> 00:11:36.030
Even at the Nobel Prize, Golgi kept saying
that Cajal was an idiot that he just screwed

00:11:36.030 --> 00:11:37.940
this up entirely.

00:11:37.940 --> 00:11:45.690
But time has allowed him to win out here.

00:11:45.690 --> 00:11:53.580
Let’s see the tools that you use to get
beyond.

00:11:53.580 --> 00:12:01.160
So the first thing we tried is if the Golgi
technique is good and every small subset of

00:12:01.160 --> 00:12:05.590
cells are labeled black and you can learn
something about the circuits, what if we could

00:12:05.590 --> 00:12:11.700
see every single cell and instead of black,
we made every cell a different color?

00:12:11.700 --> 00:12:18.880
We called this technique BrainBow and it’s
been extraordinarily popular as screensavers

00:12:18.880 --> 00:12:21.730
for those of you…

00:12:21.730 --> 00:12:30.440
yeah it’s a very powerful technique of labeling
every cell a different color and in certain

00:12:30.440 --> 00:12:33.970
parts of the nervous system, it really does
reveal circuits.

00:12:33.970 --> 00:12:37.810
But when you get to the cerebral cortex, guess
what?

00:12:37.810 --> 00:12:44.610
There’s just so many wires there that you
can’t actually tell the colors apart because

00:12:44.610 --> 00:12:50.940
even at the finest resolution of the light
microscope and there’s a picture of those

00:12:50.940 --> 00:12:56.450
big blobs are nerve cells and all that stuff
in between, that felt work, are the bazillion

00:12:56.450 --> 00:13:01.670
wires that attach them and you can't resolve
them as individuals.

00:13:01.670 --> 00:13:04.580
So yeah we have to go to a different technique.

00:13:04.580 --> 00:13:11.020
So that’s plan A, so plan B is what we’re
working on now which is to do this, not with

00:13:11.020 --> 00:13:17.570
light and color, but use electrons which have
a much shorter wavelength and give us the

00:13:17.570 --> 00:13:20.780
ability to resolve every one of those wires.

00:13:20.780 --> 00:13:25.460
And the way we do this is we take a block
of the brain and we fill it with a metal called

00:13:25.460 --> 00:13:27.560
osmium.

00:13:27.560 --> 00:13:32.690
And we take that block of brain filled with
Osmium, that little black spot in there and

00:13:32.690 --> 00:13:38.740
then we put it in plastic, really hard plastic
and we put it on a chuck and we move it up

00:13:38.740 --> 00:13:41.000
and down, against a diamond knife.

00:13:41.000 --> 00:13:48.160
That’s what’s going on in that second
picture, and then from there, out comes one

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:49.470
section after another.

00:13:49.470 --> 00:13:55.370
They’re about a thousandth as thick as a
human hair so it has a thousandth of a hair

00:13:55.370 --> 00:13:56.500
thickness.

00:13:56.500 --> 00:14:02.040
Then we pick them up on a tape and then we
take a picture of each and every one of them

00:14:02.040 --> 00:14:04.540
with a very high-resolution microscope.

00:14:04.540 --> 00:14:11.670
And the pictures are 250,000 pixels by 250,000
pixels for each image.

00:14:11.670 --> 00:14:20.460
And then we need to take 33,333 sections like
that to do a cubic millimeter.

00:14:20.460 --> 00:14:21.460
So that’s what----

00:14:21.460 --> 00:14:29.480
So you take that level of resolution in 2D
and do 33,333 to make it 3D.

00:14:29.480 --> 00:14:30.480
Right.

00:14:30.480 --> 00:14:34.650
So it’s basically a film strip, and you
take those pictures and you stack them up

00:14:34.650 --> 00:14:40.060
and you get a stack of images that are now
a three dimensional image of the brain and

00:14:40.060 --> 00:14:47.180
then you color in the objects using deep convolutional
neural nets to generate the structures of

00:14:47.180 --> 00:14:48.180
the brain.

00:14:48.180 --> 00:14:54.630
Here is a dendrite of a nerve cell and an
axon talking to that cell in green and that’s

00:14:54.630 --> 00:14:59.460
just one piece of an area we did, which is
just so discouraging.

00:14:59.460 --> 00:15:01.040
It’s so depressing.

00:15:01.040 --> 00:15:05.590
There’s just so much stuff in these brains!

00:15:05.590 --> 00:15:08.190
There’s 1500 nerve cells.

00:15:08.190 --> 00:15:10.920
This is three billionths of a mouse brain.

00:15:10.920 --> 00:15:15.730
It took us five years to do three billionths
of a mouse brain when we started.

00:15:15.730 --> 00:15:21.290
It’s just extraordinary: there’s a synapse
every cubic micron so there are 1500 synapses

00:15:21.290 --> 00:15:28.820
in there so… yeah…

00:15:28.820 --> 00:15:36.009
I mean…

00:15:36.009 --> 00:15:40.623
I could say this to all four of you but really,
my response is just ‘I am not worthy.

00:15:40.623 --> 00:15:42.279
I am not worthy.’

00:15:42.279 --> 00:15:52.540
I mean, do you ever think about how fifty
years from now, a hundred years from now,

00:15:52.540 --> 00:15:54.740
how they will look back on this work and---

00:15:54.740 --> 00:15:57.180
As primitive and stupid.

00:15:57.180 --> 00:16:03.750
It’s like the early days of genomics when
people actually used their thumbs and pipette

00:16:03.750 --> 00:16:08.080
men to get the DNA sequence of a virus.

00:16:08.080 --> 00:16:13.279
And now we have these robots who can do a
whole genome in a day or two for a few thousand

00:16:13.279 --> 00:16:14.760
dollars.

00:16:14.760 --> 00:16:17.990
We are now back at the virus stage.

00:16:17.990 --> 00:16:20.660
We’re just starting.

00:16:20.660 --> 00:16:26.920
Nim, where would you say, in terms of the
two parts that we’re going to be discussing,

00:16:26.920 --> 00:16:32.779
functional and structural sort of aspects
of the macro-brain.

00:16:32.779 --> 00:16:36.160
Where are the scientific disagreements within
that?

00:16:36.160 --> 00:16:39.620
What are the arguments, what are the big questions?

00:16:39.620 --> 00:16:43.141
Yeah, I mean I think when just looking at
the people we have on the panel here, one

00:16:43.141 --> 00:16:49.240
question we want to know is are the data that
we’re getting on the level that Jeff’s

00:16:49.240 --> 00:16:54.980
seeing comparable to the types of level we’re
getting with FMRI and what is FMRI measuring

00:16:54.980 --> 00:16:55.980
and…

00:16:55.980 --> 00:16:57.279
What does that stand for, FMRI?

00:16:57.279 --> 00:17:00.180
Functional Magnetic Resonance Imaging so it’s
the-

00:17:00.180 --> 00:17:01.430
So what’s that taking a picture of?

00:17:01.430 --> 00:17:06.000
It’s the data that gives you the activation
patterns on the brain, so what we commonly

00:17:06.000 --> 00:17:09.160
think of as the blobs on the brain.

00:17:09.160 --> 00:17:15.540
And is that… the pictures of the neuron
electricity?

00:17:15.540 --> 00:17:22.870
So it’s not electricity, it’s an area
of active inquiry to try and understand what

00:17:22.870 --> 00:17:24.470
is the bold signal?

00:17:24.470 --> 00:17:29.660
So when you’re seeing those colored pictures,
what you’re getting is a statistical map

00:17:29.660 --> 00:17:38.520
of a signal called Bold, which is giving us
an index of where oxygen is being distributed

00:17:38.520 --> 00:17:43.680
differentially across the brain as the brain
is engaging in a particular task.

00:17:43.680 --> 00:17:52.800
So its data that presumes that a mapping of
blood activity and oxygenated blood activity

00:17:52.800 --> 00:17:59.220
in the brain is comparable to neuronal and
cognitive, well I mean not cognitive, that’s

00:17:59.220 --> 00:18:03.170
too much to say but electrochemical activity
in the brain, is that fair to say?

00:18:03.170 --> 00:18:04.360
Right, it’s an inference.

00:18:04.360 --> 00:18:07.370
We can only make an inference with those data.

00:18:07.370 --> 00:18:10.490
How good is that inference, Jeff?

00:18:10.490 --> 00:18:19.660
The challenge is in one little blob of the
FMRI image, you have fifty thousand nerve

00:18:19.660 --> 00:18:27.040
cells and a billion synapses from hundreds
of different cell types over many different

00:18:27.040 --> 00:18:30.120
layers of cerebral cortex.

00:18:30.120 --> 00:18:33.840
Each part of the cerebral cortex is layered
and there are different kinds of things going

00:18:33.840 --> 00:18:39.930
on in each layer and all that is compacted
into a single dot.

00:18:39.930 --> 00:18:41.160
On an FMRI image.

00:18:41.160 --> 00:18:45.980
Yes, so it is a global average.

00:18:45.980 --> 00:18:51.940
I would say if you tried to do the equivalent
of an FMRI on the United States, you would

00:18:51.940 --> 00:18:56.340
be looking at energy use in cities.

00:18:56.340 --> 00:18:57.340
Perhaps.

00:18:57.340 --> 00:18:58.340
Sheboygan?

00:18:58.340 --> 00:19:00.390
Yeah Sheboygan maybe.

00:19:00.390 --> 00:19:02.890
You might be able to get a little bit of Sheboygan
data.

00:19:02.890 --> 00:19:05.400
You’d get more data from bigger cities.

00:19:05.400 --> 00:19:09.030
And these cities have a lot of energy use
in the daytime.

00:19:09.030 --> 00:19:12.650
They fall off at night, but it’s not like
nothing is going on.

00:19:12.650 --> 00:19:18.470
But you would not be looking at each individual
person or each individual activity.

00:19:18.470 --> 00:19:23.380
You would be looking at essentially all the
lights on in a particular part of the brain

00:19:23.380 --> 00:19:24.380
at a particular time.

00:19:24.380 --> 00:19:29.460
And so you can infer something, I’m not
saying you can’t infer something, but there

00:19:29.460 --> 00:19:37.080
is still a chasm between the machine that’s
doing the work that is very, very intricate,

00:19:37.080 --> 00:19:41.800
and these rather global macroscopic techniques
that can work in a living human brain.

00:19:41.800 --> 00:19:45.970
I can’t-if you want me to do this to your
brain, I have to kill you.

00:19:45.970 --> 00:19:46.970
So.

00:19:46.970 --> 00:19:52.550
I’m scared of you most of all Jeff, that’s
for sure.

00:19:52.550 --> 00:19:56.680
And Deanna, FMRI, useful tool but there’s
a huge horizon beyond it.

00:19:56.680 --> 00:19:57.700
That’s fair to say?

00:19:57.700 --> 00:19:59.050
Yes, absolutely.

00:19:59.050 --> 00:20:00.710
And David, you would go along with that?

00:20:00.710 --> 00:20:02.110
We don’t need to belabor this point.

00:20:02.110 --> 00:20:06.640
I would also agree strongly and I would point
out there’s another type of information

00:20:06.640 --> 00:20:10.661
that is very important for the Human Connectome
Project and other endeavors which is what

00:20:10.661 --> 00:20:17.530
we call diffusion imaging and looking at structural
connectivity and that is also a very informative

00:20:17.530 --> 00:20:18.530
method.

00:20:18.530 --> 00:20:24.390
It relies on the preferred diffusion of water
along the length of axons within the white

00:20:24.390 --> 00:20:28.520
matter and it’s a little bit faster than
trying to get across the membranes of each

00:20:28.520 --> 00:20:29.560
axon.

00:20:29.560 --> 00:20:35.400
That indirectly informs us about local orientations
of fibers bundles that can then be stitched

00:20:35.400 --> 00:20:41.370
together to estimate long distance connections
from one region of grey matter to another.

00:20:41.370 --> 00:20:47.280
That is also fraught with its own challenges
because it’s looking at bundles that are

00:20:47.280 --> 00:20:53.670
criss-crossing one another to reflect the
actual reality of brain circuitry and we can

00:20:53.670 --> 00:20:58.750
make estimates, but they’re not perfect
and so another of the hot debates in the field

00:20:58.750 --> 00:21:03.920
is what can we infer that we can put confidence
in and what do we have to be very careful

00:21:03.920 --> 00:21:04.920
of and not over-interpret?

00:21:04.920 --> 00:21:09.730
But I have to say David, those are some damn
pretty pictures.

00:21:09.730 --> 00:21:11.990
Those are great.

00:21:11.990 --> 00:21:18.720
Why spend so much time and money mapping every
single connection if we might never be able

00:21:18.720 --> 00:21:20.280
to finish it?

00:21:20.280 --> 00:21:21.280
Jeff?

00:21:21.280 --> 00:21:28.940
You know I think that everyone probably would
agree that the Human Genome Project was worthwhile.

00:21:28.940 --> 00:21:36.580
This was a descriptive attempt to get the
nucleotide sequence down to every single nucleotide

00:21:36.580 --> 00:21:39.630
in the DNA of a human being.

00:21:39.630 --> 00:21:44.490
And when that project was first proposed,
there were a lot of people who said, “Why

00:21:44.490 --> 00:21:50.170
should we spend all this money to get all
this DNA; we know a lot of it is junk DNA,

00:21:50.170 --> 00:21:53.340
there’s a lot of things in there we don’t
care about.

00:21:53.340 --> 00:21:54.480
Why do this?

00:21:54.480 --> 00:21:57.960
This is just going to be impossible to understand.”

00:21:57.960 --> 00:22:04.130
But no one in the scientific community at
least doubts the extraordinary value of actually

00:22:04.130 --> 00:22:09.670
having a data set that describes the human
genome, and not only that, but now that we

00:22:09.670 --> 00:22:14.559
have the way to do it, we can get your genome
and my genome and they’re different.

00:22:14.559 --> 00:22:18.000
And the genome of people who have diseases
are a little different.

00:22:18.000 --> 00:22:25.670
A profound amount of insight has come from
generating that data set and I see connectomics

00:22:25.670 --> 00:22:30.960
just like how I see genomics: it is a way
to describe the brain at enough resolution

00:22:30.960 --> 00:22:36.741
that we can begin to make reasonable hypotheses
about how brains of people who have disease

00:22:36.741 --> 00:22:41.420
are different from brains of people who don’t
have diseases of the brain for example.

00:22:41.420 --> 00:22:44.530
How baby brains are wired differently than
adult brains.

00:22:44.530 --> 00:22:50.790
And old people, even old people who don’t
have dementia, and my children would count

00:22:50.790 --> 00:22:54.650
me in this category, are a little different
from young people.

00:22:54.650 --> 00:22:57.000
We’re a little more set in our ways.

00:22:57.000 --> 00:22:58.160
Why is that?

00:22:58.160 --> 00:23:00.330
Where does that change take place?

00:23:00.330 --> 00:23:02.150
Where are all our memories stored?

00:23:02.150 --> 00:23:06.790
There are millions and millions of questions
where this kind of data would be justifiable.

00:23:06.790 --> 00:23:16.540
By analogy to the genome, how much variability
do you expect from in the connectome?”

00:23:16.540 --> 00:23:18.640
Does that question make sense?

00:23:18.640 --> 00:23:22.800
L Yes, that’s a great question.

00:23:22.800 --> 00:23:28.660
Obviously there are five of us sitting here
and we all have a different set of experiences

00:23:28.660 --> 00:23:32.250
that have molded us into the adults we are.

00:23:32.250 --> 00:23:36.620
People like me believe that information is
embedded in our wiring diagrams so it would

00:23:36.620 --> 00:23:41.880
be shocking if our wiring diagrams are very
similar.

00:23:41.880 --> 00:23:47.390
Now, it’s true also that genomes are not
identical, otherwise we would all look the

00:23:47.390 --> 00:23:51.020
same, which would be very scary I think.

00:23:51.020 --> 00:23:58.820
But there’s no way the connectome variability
would be on the order of chimps have 99% of

00:23:58.820 --> 00:24:03.080
the same genetic information it appears that
humans do.

00:24:03.080 --> 00:24:04.180
I think that this is a good question.

00:24:04.180 --> 00:24:06.981
It may be depending on what level of resolution
you look at.

00:24:06.981 --> 00:24:12.510
If you look at the major pathways that connect
parts of the brain, things that come out of

00:24:12.510 --> 00:24:18.570
the BOLD technique and perhaps at diffusor
tensor imaging, you might find that brains

00:24:18.570 --> 00:24:21.380
actually are 95% similar.

00:24:21.380 --> 00:24:24.420
I actually don’t know but probably they’re
quite similar.

00:24:24.420 --> 00:24:29.260
If you get down to the wiring of a little
piece of cerebral cortex, we would probably

00:24:29.260 --> 00:24:34.760
see a lot of substantial differences between
the brains of different people.

00:24:34.760 --> 00:24:35.760
Deanna.

00:24:35.760 --> 00:24:39.450
Well I was going to say I think this it's
kind of the glass half empty, glass half full

00:24:39.450 --> 00:24:44.050
and I think this is one of the interesting
things that’s coming out of the data produced

00:24:44.050 --> 00:24:47.640
by the human connectome project because one
of our explicit goals was to look at individual

00:24:47.640 --> 00:24:50.950
differences and relate them to brain or behavior.

00:24:50.950 --> 00:24:55.950
And I think we’re seeing exactly that, that
there is remarkable similarity in a sort of

00:24:55.950 --> 00:25:03.450
basic network structure, at the level of Sheboygan
and Chicago and New York but there are also

00:25:03.450 --> 00:25:08.240
really interesting differences, that seem
to relate to behavior, including a lot of

00:25:08.240 --> 00:25:14.890
those sort of high level cognitive and emotional
and motivational behaviors.

00:25:14.890 --> 00:25:19.510
So I think we’re going to see it’s both,
that there’s real commonality in key structures,

00:25:19.510 --> 00:25:22.840
but really important individual differences
that link to behavior.

00:25:22.840 --> 00:25:27.580
I think many people use this as a strong argument
against doing this.

00:25:27.580 --> 00:25:31.070
Well if everybody’s brain is different,
what are you going to learn from doing just

00:25:31.070 --> 00:25:32.070
one brain?

00:25:32.070 --> 00:25:36.429
Well it’s a bit like saying what could you
learn, if every city is different---

00:25:36.429 --> 00:25:41.070
Why teach all the kids?

00:25:41.070 --> 00:25:42.070
Right.

00:25:42.070 --> 00:25:44.040
There’s another example.

00:25:44.040 --> 00:25:45.990
But every city is different.

00:25:45.990 --> 00:25:54.660
But if you studied in detail Paris, I suspect
you would get insights that translate to London,

00:25:54.660 --> 00:25:56.180
to New York---

00:25:56.180 --> 00:26:03.361
David, who inspired you to do this work and
what was your original conception of a brain

00:26:03.361 --> 00:26:04.361
map?

00:26:04.361 --> 00:26:10.610
I’ve been fascinated by the cerebral cortex
for forty years and when I started working

00:26:10.610 --> 00:26:16.790
on the problem I was looking at the visual
cortex of an animal model, the macaque monkey.

00:26:16.790 --> 00:26:23.059
It has a sheet of tissue that is convoluted
to get it to fit inside the skull of a monkey

00:26:23.059 --> 00:26:29.380
and one of the challenges I faced was in order
to get information about the brain and the

00:26:29.380 --> 00:26:35.560
connections, it was necessary to take slices
of the brain and look one slice at a time.

00:26:35.560 --> 00:26:43.980
I was compelled by the analogy with maps of
the earth, that two dimensional of a convoluted

00:26:43.980 --> 00:26:49.299
sheet are very informative, so at that time,
I actually used pencil and tracing paper to

00:26:49.299 --> 00:26:55.540
make crude but useful maps of the visual cortex
and then the entire cerebral cortex of the

00:26:55.540 --> 00:26:56.720
macaque monkey.

00:26:56.720 --> 00:27:02.410
I knew this was a job better suited for a
computer and when I moved to Caltech in 1976,

00:27:02.410 --> 00:27:04.320
we started working on that problem.

00:27:04.320 --> 00:27:09.030
But it literally took two decades to get the
computer horsepower but more importantly the

00:27:09.030 --> 00:27:13.820
algorithms into operational shapes for others
to lead the way.

00:27:13.820 --> 00:27:19.160
A number of groups have worked on that effort
so now we can make computerized maps of the

00:27:19.160 --> 00:27:23.760
monkey’s cerebral cortex but more excitingly,
of the human cerebral cortex, which is even

00:27:23.760 --> 00:27:25.809
more complicated and convoluted.

00:27:25.809 --> 00:27:34.870
When you were kicking around back as an advanced
science student in high school and maybe college,

00:27:34.870 --> 00:27:41.351
what were the visuals that you could see that
would show you what the map of the brain or

00:27:41.351 --> 00:27:45.000
the functionality of the brain supposedly
was all about?

00:27:45.000 --> 00:27:49.450
They were pictures in books and it’s nice
that you brought forward exhibit A which is

00:27:49.450 --> 00:27:55.190
the classic, century old map of the human
cerebral cortex by the great German anatomist

00:27:55.190 --> 00:27:56.700
Korbinian Brodmann who published…

00:27:56.700 --> 00:27:59.630
This is the 1909 map you were talking about.

00:27:59.630 --> 00:28:00.630
That’s right.

00:28:00.630 --> 00:28:07.580
I learned about it in college and it’s still
taught today as one representation but it’s

00:28:07.580 --> 00:28:12.640
analogous to maps of the earth’s surface
from the mid sixteenth century for intents

00:28:12.640 --> 00:28:15.390
which were quaint and colorful.

00:28:15.390 --> 00:28:20.890
Brodmann, there’s a colorful version of
his but this is the map that he used and it

00:28:20.890 --> 00:28:27.150
was actually accurate to this day for a few
of the fifty other regions he identified but

00:28:27.150 --> 00:28:33.540
there was reason to suspect that this was
not the whole story and it’s been a century

00:28:33.540 --> 00:28:39.920
long endeavor to make better maps of the human
cortex.

00:28:39.920 --> 00:28:45.960
(speaking German)

00:28:45.960 --> 00:28:49.810
Your German is impeccable.

00:28:49.810 --> 00:28:52.840
1907.

00:28:52.840 --> 00:29:01.130
So, getting from these maps, you had to, and
you alluded to it a moment ago, you had to

00:29:01.130 --> 00:29:07.030
really begin to describe the meaning of the
folds in the brain.

00:29:07.030 --> 00:29:08.030
Right?

00:29:08.030 --> 00:29:09.030
That’s correct.

00:29:09.030 --> 00:29:15.470
I think of the folds in the human cerebral
cortex as analogous to the crumpling of the

00:29:15.470 --> 00:29:20.380
earth’s surface into mountains and valleys
and other geographic features.

00:29:20.380 --> 00:29:25.040
And that’s of course very important as part
of the physical substrate for understanding

00:29:25.040 --> 00:29:27.880
the lay of the land quite literally.

00:29:27.880 --> 00:29:33.190
The cerebral cortex contains about sixteen
billion neurons in each individual and it’s

00:29:33.190 --> 00:29:38.750
the interactions and communications of local
and long distance connections and functions

00:29:38.750 --> 00:29:45.179
of those neurons, that determine what we call
the parcels or cortical areas in the human

00:29:45.179 --> 00:29:47.309
cortical sheet.

00:29:47.309 --> 00:29:56.900
So is it the case that in the beginning, the
brain is smooth in an embryo and then development…

00:29:56.900 --> 00:30:04.820
In an embryo and even into the first two trimesters
of human embryonic and fetal development,

00:30:04.820 --> 00:30:08.250
its very smooth—it just gets slightly curved
in its shape.

00:30:08.250 --> 00:30:12.070
Alright so this is first trimester.

00:30:12.070 --> 00:30:13.320
Right.

00:30:13.320 --> 00:30:14.790
No real crinkling there at all.

00:30:14.790 --> 00:30:21.790
So by the beginning of the second trimester,
things have gotten into a somewhat curved

00:30:21.790 --> 00:30:29.770
shape, but in the explosion of cortical growth
in the last, the third trimester of human

00:30:29.770 --> 00:30:34.170
gestation, things get really wild and crumples
up like that.

00:30:34.170 --> 00:30:40.100
Yours is good for one individual and mine
is even more crumpled, so it’s pretty severe.

00:30:40.100 --> 00:30:45.430
So the more of this, the more of this.

00:30:45.430 --> 00:30:51.800
Well the more that can fit inside the skull,
it has to get through the birth canal.

00:30:51.800 --> 00:30:59.190
That’s one of the reasons we have to have
a highly convoluted cortex to get a pizza

00:30:59.190 --> 00:31:06.250
like sheet of tissue for each hemisphere crumpled
up to fit inside a compact skull that can

00:31:06.250 --> 00:31:07.330
get through the birth canal.

00:31:07.330 --> 00:31:14.179
But by birth, the cortical convolutions are
nearly as complex as they are in an adult,

00:31:14.179 --> 00:31:18.440
even though the brain is only one third the
size of an adult brain.

00:31:18.440 --> 00:31:26.929
It only goes to show how important the brain
is in terms of creating more complexity in

00:31:26.929 --> 00:31:28.600
a confined space.

00:31:28.600 --> 00:31:29.600
Right?

00:31:29.600 --> 00:31:31.610
You’re limited in the space.

00:31:31.610 --> 00:31:36.429
You’re unlimited in the level of complexity
that you can do.

00:31:36.429 --> 00:31:42.730
There are other limitations but this is how
the brain solves its own problem of dynamics.

00:31:42.730 --> 00:31:53.360
Nim, would you say that the maps that we have
now, the visual or tools that we have now

00:31:53.360 --> 00:32:00.520
are helping us to pose new questions or answer
more questions than they pose?

00:32:00.520 --> 00:32:02.470
Both, for sure.

00:32:02.470 --> 00:32:08.100
I mean, I think that as a developmental psychologist,
the reason why I came into this is because

00:32:08.100 --> 00:32:15.000
I was very interested in behavior and where
behavior comes from, and being able to get

00:32:15.000 --> 00:32:22.410
some tool that gives us structure and function
to attach these behaviors to changes everything.

00:32:22.410 --> 00:32:27.200
It starts to answer the question that you
raised which is: how does the environment

00:32:27.200 --> 00:32:29.799
get under the skin so to speak, right?

00:32:29.799 --> 00:32:36.860
So to use an analogy from the heart, our cardiac
tissue is the tissue and our heartbeat is

00:32:36.860 --> 00:32:40.040
the behavior that’s associated with that
tissue.

00:32:40.040 --> 00:32:46.429
So likewise, our brain is the tissue and behavior
is the output of that tissue.

00:32:46.429 --> 00:32:51.370
So if you want to understand and be able to
ask deeper questions about behaviors and where

00:32:51.370 --> 00:32:56.570
they come from and why am I different from
you and why are we similar in other ways,

00:32:56.570 --> 00:33:00.520
then being able to have this tool changes
the game.

00:33:00.520 --> 00:33:09.520
Deanna can you explain for us the great pictures
that we have of the twin, identical twin brains?

00:33:09.520 --> 00:33:10.950
These are identical twins.

00:33:10.950 --> 00:33:16.860
Well I think that the twin data does a wonderful
job of illustrating what Nim talked about

00:33:16.860 --> 00:33:21.910
in terms of both how the environment gets
into the brain and how genetics shape the

00:33:21.910 --> 00:33:22.910
brain.

00:33:22.910 --> 00:33:27.070
So when you look at the brains of twins, there
are things especially identical twins who

00:33:27.070 --> 00:33:33.380
share the vast majority, 99% of their genetic
makeup, there are clear similarities, things

00:33:33.380 --> 00:33:36.420
that are more similar about those twins than
other ones.

00:33:36.420 --> 00:33:38.180
But there are also fascinating differences.

00:33:38.180 --> 00:33:40.669
I mean, they don’t look identical.

00:33:40.669 --> 00:33:42.690
Identical is not even a word I would begin
to use.

00:33:42.690 --> 00:33:47.929
No, because there are also environmental factors
that get under the skin and into the brain

00:33:47.929 --> 00:33:54.150
that are going to shape you know how the brain
wires up, how those convolutions over time

00:33:54.150 --> 00:34:00.570
and the twins’ behavior will probably follow
and be similarly different or the same depending

00:34:00.570 --> 00:34:04.240
on how much they’ve experienced the environment
differently.

00:34:04.240 --> 00:34:10.079
And even if you take identical twins, and
no one would actually do this, and put them

00:34:10.079 --> 00:34:17.990
in identical green rooms and fed them identical
green food and gave them identical glasses

00:34:17.990 --> 00:34:24.929
of water at the exact same time everyday for
eight years, you would still get differences.

00:34:24.929 --> 00:34:27.129
You would get some differences for sure, yeah.

00:34:27.129 --> 00:34:28.359
But it would be less.

00:34:28.359 --> 00:34:29.359
It might be less.

00:34:29.359 --> 00:34:30.359
I mean, that’s actually a great question.

00:34:30.359 --> 00:34:31.359
We don’t know-

00:34:31.359 --> 00:34:32.359
It’s not a good experiment though

00:34:32.359 --> 00:34:35.300
We’re not going to do that experiment.

00:34:35.300 --> 00:34:40.220
But I think obviously to the degree that we
believe environment also shapes brain development,

00:34:40.220 --> 00:34:44.490
the more similar the environment, the more
likely it is that those things will be shaped,

00:34:44.490 --> 00:34:46.090
you know, in a similar way.

00:34:46.090 --> 00:34:51.870
But again, we can’t really do that experiment
to deliberately manipulate how similar or

00:34:51.870 --> 00:34:53.330
not similar the environments are.

00:34:53.330 --> 00:34:54.330
Go ahead, David.

00:34:54.330 --> 00:34:58.859
But since most of the cortical folding, the
development of the convolutions occur before

00:34:58.859 --> 00:35:05.490
birth, that’s actually occurring in not
quite identical but in the same womb and very

00:35:05.490 --> 00:35:08.030
similar environmental influences.

00:35:08.030 --> 00:35:16.610
So the fact that the outcome at birth of newborn
identical twins, their brains are very different,

00:35:16.610 --> 00:35:23.190
that tells us that a lot of this is driven
by subtle, perhaps epigenetic influences,

00:35:23.190 --> 00:35:28.480
and perhaps small differences in environment
lead to perturbations that lead to different

00:35:28.480 --> 00:35:29.940
folding outcomes.

00:35:29.940 --> 00:35:37.110
But the other part of what’s important here
is that even though the newborn twins or any

00:35:37.110 --> 00:35:44.010
other individual has a relatively, highly
convoluted and relatively mature looking cortex,

00:35:44.010 --> 00:35:50.470
during the expansion that occurs by a factor
of three, especially in early afterbirth,

00:35:50.470 --> 00:35:56.380
postnatal development, the regions that change
the most are the regions that we associate

00:35:56.380 --> 00:35:58.690
with higher cognitive functions.

00:35:58.690 --> 00:36:06.220
So that’s where our plasticity, our ability
to learn and develop and emerge with sophisticated

00:36:06.220 --> 00:36:12.070
concepts like drive this conversation, that’s
occurring in specialized regions of the cortex

00:36:12.070 --> 00:36:13.859
that are more plastic.

00:36:13.859 --> 00:36:20.800
They are also more vulnerable to abnormal
experience and perturbed development.

00:36:20.800 --> 00:36:27.260
So this relationship to disorders and disease
comes into play in that regard as well.

00:36:27.260 --> 00:36:33.500
During this rapid period, things like the
autism spectrum might emerge because it is

00:36:33.500 --> 00:36:42.190
such a complicated and rapid developmental
period that is not well understood physiologically.

00:36:42.190 --> 00:36:48.520
That is correct, although we know that there
is a certain genetic heritability to autism

00:36:48.520 --> 00:36:56.619
in terms of the likelihood of having an individual,
a child somewhere in the autism spectrum disorder.

00:36:56.619 --> 00:37:01.160
But it’s called the spectrum for good reason
because there’s tremendous diversity in

00:37:01.160 --> 00:37:06.840
many ways and that’s where the experience,
particularly in early childhood development

00:37:06.840 --> 00:37:14.050
is likely to shape the trajectory of individuals
and where we desperately want to understand

00:37:14.050 --> 00:37:21.320
how can we decipher what’s going on and
perhaps better treat and modify the outcome.

00:37:21.320 --> 00:37:28.310
Nim can you describe, lets extend this developmental
discussion because a clock begins, as David

00:37:28.310 --> 00:37:35.940
was sort of saying at birth and there are
various kinds of developmental pathways that

00:37:35.940 --> 00:37:41.490
are emerging at different times, and we’ve
started to separate them a little bit and

00:37:41.490 --> 00:37:46.120
really understand what race takes place at
what time.

00:37:46.120 --> 00:37:51.790
Yeah so human brain development is one of
the most fascinating things right?

00:37:51.790 --> 00:37:57.310
Because we start off, all of us, as a single
celled organism and then we all go through

00:37:57.310 --> 00:38:03.690
this miracle of development where we create
the most complicated three pounds in the universe.

00:38:03.690 --> 00:38:08.859
And we all do it more or less the same and
yet we all have beautiful, individual differences.

00:38:08.859 --> 00:38:10.670
So where did those come from?

00:38:10.670 --> 00:38:15.070
So why would mother nature go through that
whole process?

00:38:15.070 --> 00:38:16.220
Because that’s really inefficient.

00:38:16.220 --> 00:38:18.849
Metabolically, arguably, that’s incredibly
inefficient.

00:38:18.849 --> 00:38:25.870
And the reason is that it gives us what David
was talking about earlier: this incredible

00:38:25.870 --> 00:38:28.710
plasticity during development.

00:38:28.710 --> 00:38:35.560
It’s like throwing a big fishing net into
the ocean and then pulling back what you need.

00:38:35.560 --> 00:38:41.880
Because when we’re new on this planet, when
we just land on this planet, we don’t know

00:38:41.880 --> 00:38:45.280
what household we’re going to land in, we
don’t know what the climate’s going to

00:38:45.280 --> 00:38:50.250
be, we don’t know what language we’re
going to have to learn and yet we all do it.

00:38:50.250 --> 00:38:56.530
And so in many ways, in order to understand
the mature human brain, you want to understand

00:38:56.530 --> 00:39:02.440
the brain through infancy and childhood because
that’s what gives us that end product where

00:39:02.440 --> 00:39:08.060
we’re all exquisitely adapted to our environments
in which our brain grew up.

00:39:08.060 --> 00:39:14.940
But the profile of the adaptation process,
the becoming an adult, the maturation and

00:39:14.940 --> 00:39:18.490
the development of a personality, et cetera,
is actually a subtractive process.

00:39:18.490 --> 00:39:24.910
It’s a series of progressive and subtractive
or regressive processes.

00:39:24.910 --> 00:39:31.849
So if we look in the back of the brain where
the visual cortex is, you see this overshoot

00:39:31.849 --> 00:39:37.000
which I described and then this pruning away
like you prune your rose bushes, this pruning

00:39:37.000 --> 00:39:42.570
away of the synapses that your brain realizes
it doesn’t need.

00:39:42.570 --> 00:39:44.340
It’s inefficient to keep those.

00:39:44.340 --> 00:39:47.770
What are the two functions here that are being
looked at?

00:39:47.770 --> 00:39:54.910
So in the pinkish line is where we’re looking
at the back of the brain, the visual cortex,

00:39:54.910 --> 00:40:02.160
and you see that increase in synaptic density—the
density of the connections and then you get

00:40:02.160 --> 00:40:04.710
this pruning back as we get older.

00:40:04.710 --> 00:40:09.450
And then if you look at that blue line, that’s
the frontal cortex in the front of the brain

00:40:09.450 --> 00:40:14.260
and it does the same thing but you can see
that that whole curve is shifted over.

00:40:14.260 --> 00:40:22.050
So that’s reflecting that we have these
processes of increase in synapses and regression

00:40:22.050 --> 00:40:28.099
that’s happening in our brain but in hierarchical
fashion where we go from the back of the brain

00:40:28.099 --> 00:40:33.630
to the front of the brain which really reflects
the way these behaviors emerge over development,

00:40:33.630 --> 00:40:34.630
right?

00:40:34.630 --> 00:40:35.630
So we—

00:40:35.630 --> 00:40:42.680
Professor Nim, does this mean that evolutionarily,
cognitively we require visual information

00:40:42.680 --> 00:40:48.660
to inform our cognitive selves and so that’s
why cognitive development might lag behind

00:40:48.660 --> 00:40:49.660
visual?

00:40:49.660 --> 00:40:55.240
Right so brain development makes sense evolutionarily
but also within one individual’s lifetime.

00:40:55.240 --> 00:40:59.960
So first you have the sensory systems coming
on line because you need to be able to take

00:40:59.960 --> 00:41:02.260
in information from the environment.

00:41:02.260 --> 00:41:06.839
Then you’ve got motor systems to allow you
to interact with the environment.

00:41:06.839 --> 00:41:11.660
And those are necessary prerequisites in order
to do all of the cool, higher level stuff

00:41:11.660 --> 00:41:17.530
that we can do like plan for the future or
fall in love or control our emotions.

00:41:17.530 --> 00:41:20.830
Or even be blind, I mean blind people are
going through an adaptive process.

00:41:20.830 --> 00:41:21.830
Right.

00:41:21.830 --> 00:41:24.150
So we have multiple sensory systems, right?

00:41:24.150 --> 00:41:28.820
So we have this plasticity early on in life,
where we can shift resources to where it suits

00:41:28.820 --> 00:41:29.920
us best.

00:41:29.920 --> 00:41:35.240
How does learning multiple languages early
on affect brain development?

00:41:35.240 --> 00:41:36.730
Any knowledge about that?

00:41:36.730 --> 00:41:38.710
Yeah, so there’s a lot of excitement in
this area.

00:41:38.710 --> 00:41:42.820
So one is, it just speaks to the behavior
of language.

00:41:42.820 --> 00:41:49.480
So your brain will undergo Neural-Darwinism
during development.

00:41:49.480 --> 00:41:54.780
It learns based on who’s being activated,
which synapses are being activated or not.

00:41:54.780 --> 00:41:58.260
If they’re being activated, then I’m going
to keep them and if not, then I’m going

00:41:58.260 --> 00:41:59.690
to prune them away.

00:41:59.690 --> 00:42:05.100
So if I’m being exposed to both English
and Japanese as a six month old, then my brain

00:42:05.100 --> 00:42:06.630
is going to hang on to both of those.

00:42:06.630 --> 00:42:08.310
Is that what happened to you?

00:42:08.310 --> 00:42:09.430
Is that your experience?

00:42:09.430 --> 00:42:16.060
That was, no, I don’t speak Japanese.

00:42:16.060 --> 00:42:17.060
I wish!

00:42:17.060 --> 00:42:18.170
That in and of itself is amazing, that we’re
that plastic.

00:42:18.170 --> 00:42:20.859
So I can’t speak Japanese.

00:42:20.859 --> 00:42:26.490
There are many sounds, phonemes in Japanese
that I will never be able to process.

00:42:26.490 --> 00:42:30.280
But when I was five months old, I could.

00:42:30.280 --> 00:42:33.260
But my brain decided that it didn’t need
to hang on to those anymore.

00:42:33.260 --> 00:42:35.310
So that, in and of itself is amazing.

00:42:35.310 --> 00:42:39.280
And then the second thing is that there are
all of these cascading functions, all these

00:42:39.280 --> 00:42:41.550
cascading benefits that you get.

00:42:41.550 --> 00:42:48.670
So children who can speak two language, two
languages fluently also have this amazing

00:42:48.670 --> 00:42:50.530
benefit in their prefrontal cortex.

00:42:50.530 --> 00:42:56.930
So they’re able to switch very easily in
terms of cognitive functions because they’ve

00:42:56.930 --> 00:43:02.210
had a lifetime of switching what languages
their speaking and understanding what the

00:43:02.210 --> 00:43:05.710
other person is expecting of them in terms
of language.

00:43:05.710 --> 00:43:13.570
I’ve also heard that somebody from South
Asia who spoke I think 7 languages.

00:43:13.570 --> 00:43:21.040
Her experience was that once you get past
two, three, four and five are easy.

00:43:21.040 --> 00:43:23.700
Is that… is that what the brain tells you?

00:43:23.700 --> 00:43:28.670
Right so the brain starts picking up on what
are the common principles that I need to extract

00:43:28.670 --> 00:43:35.940
from a language so as you gather three or
four, if that’s the magic number, you get

00:43:35.940 --> 00:43:41.330
a broader sense of what are the generalized
principles here that you can apply to new

00:43:41.330 --> 00:43:42.330
languages.

00:43:42.330 --> 00:43:46.830
So yeah I would say probably the second language
is the hardest to learn and then yeah after

00:43:46.830 --> 00:43:48.089
that, it’s nothing to brag about.

00:43:48.089 --> 00:43:49.140
You’re sort of just feasting.

00:43:49.140 --> 00:43:51.740
It’s nothing to brag about after that.

00:43:51.740 --> 00:43:56.830
John, one thing that’s important too is
that those effects don’t just last through

00:43:56.830 --> 00:43:57.830
childhood.

00:43:57.830 --> 00:44:01.050
There’s evidence they last for your entire
life and actually may be protective later

00:44:01.050 --> 00:44:04.730
on in life against things like cognitive decline,
even dementia.

00:44:04.730 --> 00:44:08.940
More work is needed there, but they have a
lot of evidence that it has very beneficial,

00:44:08.940 --> 00:44:10.660
long lasting effects.

00:44:10.660 --> 00:44:12.040
Is free will an illusion?

00:44:12.040 --> 00:44:16.310
In other words, is the brain the be all and
end all… this is sophisticated, the be all

00:44:16.310 --> 00:44:18.040
and end all to our decision making?

00:44:18.040 --> 00:44:22.720
When you get to the connectome, will you be
looking at a deterministic image?

00:44:22.720 --> 00:44:24.100
Anyone want to take that on?

00:44:24.100 --> 00:44:33.640
I would like to because one of the great surprises
for me when we actually began reconstructing

00:44:33.640 --> 00:44:41.390
brains at the level of resolution of the wiring
diagram is the insane density of wires and

00:44:41.390 --> 00:44:44.410
synapses in the brain.

00:44:44.410 --> 00:44:49.370
It might as well be called free will because
it is so darn complicated.

00:44:49.370 --> 00:44:54.080
It’s not that you have to find that one
place where there’s a switch which you can

00:44:54.080 --> 00:44:56.790
just flip or roll dice and you’re going
to go left or right.

00:44:56.790 --> 00:45:02.950
The whole brain is made up of billions of
elements, trillions of synapses actually that

00:45:02.950 --> 00:45:09.480
each have a certain amount of stochastic behavior
so you might as well call this free will.

00:45:09.480 --> 00:45:10.670
It is free will for us.

00:45:10.670 --> 00:45:12.200
We believe we have free will.

00:45:12.200 --> 00:45:17.530
You look down from an airplane at New York
City at night, you’ll see most people on

00:45:17.530 --> 00:45:19.650
the highways are leaving the city.

00:45:19.650 --> 00:45:22.119
You look in the morning and those people are
coming in.

00:45:22.119 --> 00:45:23.850
That doesn’t seem like free will.

00:45:23.850 --> 00:45:28.630
To someone up there, it looks like we’re
doing exactly what we’re supposed to do

00:45:28.630 --> 00:45:30.050
to make our city to work.

00:45:30.050 --> 00:45:32.210
Anyone else want to say anything on free will?

00:45:32.210 --> 00:45:37.609
I agree with Jeff, it’s in our control.

00:45:37.609 --> 00:45:38.930
What does control mean?

00:45:38.930 --> 00:45:41.160
That’s part of what we want to decipher.

00:45:41.160 --> 00:45:43.040
Will we ever understand it?

00:45:43.040 --> 00:45:51.610
Perhaps not but fundamentally the richness
of brain circuitry gives us, in practical

00:45:51.610 --> 00:45:57.160
terms, “free will” that is our handle
on that concept.

00:45:57.160 --> 00:46:04.460
Do you believe brain data including but not
limited to the connectome can be faithfully

00:46:04.460 --> 00:46:07.550
recreated in a Von Neumann machine.

00:46:07.550 --> 00:46:14.950
Now a Von Neumann machine, is something like
a Turing machine, a theoretical idea of a

00:46:14.950 --> 00:46:24.200
computer that would reach a level of complexity
that would be able to behave comparably to

00:46:24.200 --> 00:46:26.630
what we’d think of as autonomous.

00:46:26.630 --> 00:46:33.530
I get this question a lot, that if you get
the wiring diagram, aren’t you 90% of the

00:46:33.530 --> 00:46:36.640
way there to having a thinking machine?

00:46:36.640 --> 00:46:43.030
The wiring diagram is a static entity at the
moment you take it and it doesn’t tell you

00:46:43.030 --> 00:46:45.620
the way information is flowing through those
wires.

00:46:45.620 --> 00:46:52.820
This was one of the great discoveries of neurobiology:
that information flows in a direction through

00:46:52.820 --> 00:46:54.580
individual nerve cells.

00:46:54.580 --> 00:46:59.109
That information is often stimulated by things
from the outside that are activating your

00:46:59.109 --> 00:47:03.410
retina or cells in your ear or skin.

00:47:03.410 --> 00:47:05.910
None of that is build into these wiring diagrams.

00:47:05.910 --> 00:47:07.760
That’s coming from the outside.

00:47:07.760 --> 00:47:14.339
And so when we look at the wiring diagram,
we don’t see what is actually going on.

00:47:14.339 --> 00:47:20.441
At the level of fMRI, at least you see areas
of the brain lighting up, but at the fine

00:47:20.441 --> 00:47:26.890
level of the way the machine works, we haven’t
found a way of putting information into those

00:47:26.890 --> 00:47:32.099
wires in a way that simulates the way actual
experience goes into the brain.

00:47:32.099 --> 00:47:33.970
David, you were going to say something.

00:47:33.970 --> 00:47:35.552
Do you remember what it is here you were going
to say?

00:47:35.552 --> 00:47:41.400
It was a follow up to the question about autonomous
agents and artificial intelligence.

00:47:41.400 --> 00:47:52.570
And one thing I’ve learned through my career
is never say never too earnestly because some

00:47:52.570 --> 00:47:56.500
of the things that we’re talking about is
everyday scientific experiences.

00:47:56.500 --> 00:48:03.599
We’re out of the realm of what I could even
imagine, let alone say that would be possible

00:48:03.599 --> 00:48:05.050
someday.

00:48:05.050 --> 00:48:10.339
Technology advances are driven by the laws
of physics but they can make profound advances.

00:48:10.339 --> 00:48:17.050
So some of the things we’re talking about
up here right now were impossible to imagine

00:48:17.050 --> 00:48:19.560
when you were an undergraduate.

00:48:19.560 --> 00:48:21.390
That is correct.

00:48:21.390 --> 00:48:27.440
Even magnetic resonance imagining, I remember
thinking about, would there be some way to

00:48:27.440 --> 00:48:32.500
measure brain activity and talking to experts
and it didn’t seem like it was going to

00:48:32.500 --> 00:48:34.990
go anywhere in the early 1980s.

00:48:34.990 --> 00:48:42.880
That was lack of vision and insight about
how the clever ways into the new technologies

00:48:42.880 --> 00:48:47.500
could be achieved by physicists and engineers.

00:48:47.500 --> 00:48:51.550
But I was also going to come back to the point
about artificial intelligence.

00:48:51.550 --> 00:48:58.310
Many people know about entities like Watson
and other extraordinarily sophisticated computers

00:48:58.310 --> 00:49:06.740
which, in some contexts can do impressively
well in communicating with humans and giving

00:49:06.740 --> 00:49:10.810
the appearance of intelligent thought.

00:49:10.810 --> 00:49:14.230
Arguably there is some degree of intelligence
there.

00:49:14.230 --> 00:49:19.420
I don’t think Watson now would be able to
sit at this discussion with a fifth chair

00:49:19.420 --> 00:49:26.570
or sixth chair and carry his or her digital
weight in this kind of free flowing conversation.

00:49:26.570 --> 00:49:34.900
But I’ve heard Watson described one time
as a combination of the Library of Congress

00:49:34.900 --> 00:49:41.790
and sprinter Usain Bolt, running around getting
source material---

00:49:41.790 --> 00:49:44.420
Right, working in a profoundly different way.

00:49:44.420 --> 00:49:49.050
So will there be versions of artificial intelligence
in the future that are dramatically better

00:49:49.050 --> 00:49:50.780
than Watson?

00:49:50.780 --> 00:49:53.240
Unquestionably in my mind.

00:49:53.240 --> 00:49:59.510
Will that ever come close to the cleverness
and insight and perspective of real human

00:49:59.510 --> 00:50:02.180
beings having a conversation like this?

00:50:02.180 --> 00:50:05.980
I personally am on the skeptical side.

00:50:05.980 --> 00:50:08.910
I wouldn’t say impossible, but I’m doubtful.

00:50:08.910 --> 00:50:15.400
And I think if they are doing it, one of the
fascinating challenges is to what degree will

00:50:15.400 --> 00:50:19.960
computers emulate human-like thought strategies?

00:50:19.960 --> 00:50:28.430
To what extent will they use principles of
neural-circuitry like what Jeff focuses on,

00:50:28.430 --> 00:50:35.820
to process information in ways that emulate
the characteristics that Deanna, Nim and I

00:50:35.820 --> 00:50:37.580
look at in the living human brain.

00:50:37.580 --> 00:50:43.030
A huge, open and exciting question and it’ll
come back to the next generation of young

00:50:43.030 --> 00:50:46.050
scientists who carry that ball forward.

00:50:46.050 --> 00:50:55.109
How can we continue to develop new synaptic
responses as an adult?

00:50:55.109 --> 00:51:01.599
So we do, we are.

00:51:01.599 --> 00:51:07.510
The fact that we can learn new things, the
fact that we can form more memories depends

00:51:07.510 --> 00:51:12.970
on these microstructural things happening,
otherwise we would have no memory.

00:51:12.970 --> 00:51:17.230
So we have incredible plasticity early in
life.

00:51:17.230 --> 00:51:20.250
It decreases but it never goes away permanently.

00:51:20.250 --> 00:51:25.540
Some argue that in some areas of the prefrontal
cortex we actually never reach our “full

00:51:25.540 --> 00:51:29.960
mature state” because we’re constantly
exhibiting high levels of plasticity.

00:51:29.960 --> 00:51:37.089
So, some of the exciting interventions are
things like ‘how can exercise boost that

00:51:37.089 --> 00:51:40.540
even further for individuals who are experiencing
cognitive decline?

00:51:40.540 --> 00:51:44.790
Or how can pharmacological agents increase
that plasticity further?”

00:51:44.790 --> 00:51:46.180
Career changing millennials.

00:51:46.180 --> 00:51:47.500
Career changing millennials.

00:51:47.500 --> 00:51:48.500
Sleep.

00:51:48.500 --> 00:51:51.980
All of the things we want to know if they’re
good for us are probably having an effect

00:51:51.980 --> 00:51:53.980
on increasing our plasticity.

00:51:53.980 --> 00:51:54.980
Deanna?

00:51:54.980 --> 00:51:59.140
Yeah, I was just going to comment on that
and sleep and you know there are fascinating

00:51:59.140 --> 00:52:06.150
theories on sleep is sort of a way to clean
up synapses so you can do more the next day.

00:52:06.150 --> 00:52:10.560
There’s fascinating work looking at brain
injuries and ways that rehabilitation can

00:52:10.560 --> 00:52:14.050
help with, you know, rewiring the brain.

00:52:14.050 --> 00:52:17.330
So I think that there’s a lot of hope that
it will continue on.

00:52:17.330 --> 00:52:20.310
It’s less but it still continues.

00:52:20.310 --> 00:52:21.560
Jeff?

00:52:21.560 --> 00:52:27.980
Well, you know, I hate to say this because
it’s a downer but being an old person and

00:52:27.980 --> 00:52:34.800
I’d like to think that I’m infinitely
flexible, but I remember when I was young,

00:52:34.800 --> 00:52:42.050
being impressed at how narrow-minded my parents
were when they were my age.

00:52:42.050 --> 00:52:47.240
And I have a feeling that my children think
a little bit about me this way.

00:52:47.240 --> 00:52:53.670
So although I feel plastic, I think the world
is beginning to assume I just always talk

00:52:53.670 --> 00:52:56.320
about the same thing over and over again.

00:52:56.320 --> 00:53:00.470
I say it’s because it’s really interesting
but it’s probably because all of the other

00:53:00.470 --> 00:53:06.099
things my circuits could have done have been
pruned away.

00:53:06.099 --> 00:53:09.500
Rust.

00:53:09.500 --> 00:53:14.080
All we are is rust.

00:53:14.080 --> 00:53:18.090
But I mean, if you took up the violin or something…

00:53:18.090 --> 00:53:20.109
I wouldn’t.

00:53:20.109 --> 00:53:26.380
I’m so glad I didn’t end on Jeff.

00:53:26.380 --> 00:53:28.720
David?

00:53:28.720 --> 00:53:33.500
Well I see a lot of younger faces out in the
audience and I would reiterate the point you

00:53:33.500 --> 00:53:42.050
guys are the future of those of us who are
above fifty, to put it mildly.

00:53:42.050 --> 00:53:47.910
We will continue to work hard and look for
opportunities to strengthen in plasticity,

00:53:47.910 --> 00:53:50.109
in healthy aging.

00:53:50.109 --> 00:53:55.740
We will continue to chip away at the profound
impact of brain disorders and diseases, which

00:53:55.740 --> 00:54:01.130
is a scourge of society and we desperately
need better solutions.

00:54:01.130 --> 00:54:09.570
And we are excited by the interest in the
brain and neuroscience manifested by the wonderful

00:54:09.570 --> 00:54:15.190
attendance at this session and we look forward
to future years and reading and learning about

00:54:15.190 --> 00:54:18.740
the accomplishments of the next generation,
so thanks to all.

